@meta
id: 30-03
title: The index that lives in a file
chapter: 30
type: lesson
skills: ctags
par: 53
minutes: 6
vscode: Ctrl-T, the symbol search box
vimrc: set hidden
open: src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts

@brief
    REF-204  PaymentService throws REFUND_FAILD. The error table has no
             such code, so the handler falls through to a 500.

One misspelled string. You do not know which file it is in, and there is
no search to run yet because you do not know the file either.

There is a `tags` file in the root of this tree. It is a plain text
index: one line per definition, giving the name, the file it lives in,
and how to find it once you are there. `:tag issueRefund` reads it and
puts you on the definition, wherever that is. The file is provided with
this lesson — normally `ctags -R src` writes it in about a second.

Fix the code so it reads `REFUND_FAILED`. Leave the tags file alone.

@keys
| `:tag name` | jump to the definition of `name` |
| `:tag /pattern` | jump to the first definition matching a pattern |
| `:tags` | the stack of tag jumps you have made |
| `'tags'` | the option naming the tags files vim reads |

@hints
`:tag issueRefund`. You do not have to be in the right file, or know
which file the right file is.
--
Once you are on the definition, the typo is a short search away and
`ciw` replaces the whole word.
--
`:tag issueRefund` `Enter`, `/FAILD` `Enter`, `ciwREFUND_FAILED` `Esc`,
`:wqa` `Enter`.

@solution
    :tag issueRefund<Enter>  /FAILD<Enter>  ciw REFUND_FAILED <Esc>  :wqa<Enter>

Open the `tags` file and read it — it is not magic. Each line is

    name   <tab>   file   <tab>   address

where the address is either a line number or a search pattern that
finds the definition again after the file has been edited around it.
That last detail is why a slightly stale tags file still works: it
searches, so a definition that has moved down twenty lines is still
found.

`ctags -R src` builds one for a whole project. It understands about
forty languages, it takes a second on a repo that a language server
needs a minute to index, and the result is one file you can commit,
rsync, or generate on a machine you have only ssh access to.

A tags file has no idea about types, scope or imports. It is a phone
book: names to places. The whole of the next three lessons is about
getting good value out of exactly that much information — and the
lesson after those is about the one thing it gets wrong.

@start tags
!_TAG_FILE_FORMAT	2	//
!_TAG_FILE_SORTED	1	//
HttpClient	src/infrastructure/http/http.client.ts	/^export class HttpClient {$/;"	c
OrderResolver	src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts	/^export class OrderResolver {$/;"	c
PaymentService	src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts	/^export class PaymentService {$/;"	c
issueRefund	src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts	/^  public async issueRefund(orderId: string) {$/;"	m	class:PaymentService
post	src/infrastructure/http/http.client.ts	/^  public async post(path: string, body: unknown) {$/;"	m	class:HttpClient

@start src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async cancelOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id);
  }
}

@start src/infrastructure/http/http.client.ts
export class HttpClient {
  public async post(path: string, body: unknown) {
    return fetch(path, { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(body) });
  }
}

@start src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { HttpClient } from '../http/http.client';

export class PaymentService {
  constructor(protected http: HttpClient) {}

  public async issueRefund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/refunds', { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILD');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}

@expected src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { HttpClient } from '../http/http.client';

export class PaymentService {
  constructor(protected http: HttpClient) {}

  public async issueRefund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post('/refunds', { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}
